From ce8e118cdcb67ba4d0795aab904bab4e79717cb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:32:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper_pbr: policy routing follows the order the rules are in

FIRST MATCH WINS. Every selector rule now carries `-m mark --mark 0x0/$MASK`,
so the first rule to match a packet is the only one that writes a verdict.

Before this, every matching rule ran and the LAST one in the list decided:
MARK does not terminate a chain and --set-xmark overwrites the masked nibble,
and the only RETURN in REAPER_PBR sits above the selectors to honour a
restored CONNMARK verdict. So a broad source rule placed below a specific
ipset or domain rule silently overwrote it.

Field report 2026-09-17 (RT-BE86U, v3.1.8_BETA): a locally attached client
sent to a tunnel by a Policy Routing SOURCE rule stopped following its own
ipset destination rules, while the same client sent to the same tunnel by
VPN Director kept following them - which made it look like a VPN fault. It
was not: VPN Director's source rule is an `ip rule` at pref 10210 while the
destination marks resolve at 9002, so the lower pref wins there. Inside
REAPER_PBR both rules were mark writes in one chain, and position decided.

The reporter's chain had eight destination rules followed by one source rule,
so the source rule beat all eight. With this change their list works as
written, unchanged. Put specific rules above broad ones.

Behaviour change: a list that depended on the old last-match-wins order will
route differently. The mark rule COUNT is unchanged, so rwatch's
`grep -c 'MARK --set'` reassert check and the diag figure are unaffected, and
the walker already models an `-m mark` match so no witness row goes unmodelled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/rc/reaper_pbr.c | 16 ++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/reaper_pbr.c b/release/src/router/rc/reaper_pbr.c
index 603c30f745..bd49fc000d 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/reaper_pbr.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/reaper_pbr.c
@@ -26,6 +26,18 @@
  *                   purpose - an explicit PBR rule is evaluated FIRST and thus
  *                   OVERRIDES VPN Director's broad from/to rules (the intended
  *                   precedence: PBR is the specific override).
+ *   rule order    : FIRST MATCH WINS (v3.1.9). Every selector rule carries
+ *                   `-m mark --mark 0x0/$MASK`, so once a rule has marked a
+ *                   packet no later rule can overwrite it. Before v3.1.9
+ *                   every matching rule ran and the LAST in the list won -
+ *                   MARK does not terminate and --set-xmark overwrites - so
+ *                   a broad source rule sitting BELOW a specific ipset rule
+ *                   silently beat it. Field report 2026-09-17: a client sent
+ *                   to a tunnel by a PBR source rule stopped following its
+ *                   ipset destination rules, while the same client sent by
+ *                   VPN Director still did - that path resolves by ip-rule
+ *                   pref, which is unaffected. Put specific rules ABOVE
+ *                   broad ones; the list is emitted in its stored order.
  *   tables        : the EXISTING per-tunnel tables ovpnc1..5 / wgc1..5 (VPN
  *                   Director and the WG client own them); WAN = table main;
  *                   BLOCK = prohibit. No new tables.
@@ -593,14 +605,14 @@ static int ppbr_gen(const char *path, int teardown_only, const ppbr_cfg_t *cfg)
 						 * minutes for the life of the box - each pass taking the
 						 * firewall lock and issuing an fc flush. */
 						fprintf(rb, "ipset list -n rwfw_%s >/dev/null 2>&1 && ", ent);
-					fprintf(rb, "pbr_rule iptables -t mangle -A " PPBR_CHAIN " %s -j MARK --set-xmark 0x%X0000/$MASK\n",
+					fprintf(rb, "pbr_rule iptables -t mangle -A " PPBR_CHAIN " -m mark --mark 0x0/$MASK %s -j MARK --set-xmark 0x%X0000/$MASK\n",
 					        sel4, code);
 				}
 				if (sel6[0]) {
 					if (!strncmp(sel6, "-m set", 6))
 						/* only when the v6 set exists (see ppbr_selector) */
 						fprintf(rb, "ipset list -n rwfw6_%s >/dev/null 2>&1 && ", ent);
-					fprintf(rb, "pbr_rule ip6tables -t mangle -A " PPBR_CHAIN " %s -j MARK --set-xmark 0x%X0000/$MASK\n",
+					fprintf(rb, "pbr_rule ip6tables -t mangle -A " PPBR_CHAIN " -m mark --mark 0x0/$MASK %s -j MARK --set-xmark 0x%X0000/$MASK\n",
 					        sel6, code);
 				}
 				if (wg) {
